rlm-bq-archive

Multi-select BigQuery tables from the bq-open cache, run three
diagnostic queries per table (recent jobs, audit-log access, storage
freshness), print the results, ask for confirmation, then copy the
selected table(s) into the archival_data dataset with
`bq cp --no-clobber`, verify each copy, and offer to delete each
verified source table one at a time.

Selected entries are first classified via `bq show`. Only actual
tables (TABLE / EXTERNAL / SNAPSHOT) go through the diagnostics and
copy.

VIEWS ARE OFFERED FOR DELETION INSTEAD OF ARCHIVING

A view stores no data of its own — it is a saved SELECT — so there is
nothing for `bq cp` to copy and an "archived" view would only be a
detached duplicate of the definition. Selecting one is therefore read
as "I want this gone": VIEW and MATERIALIZED_VIEW entries skip the
diagnostics, confirmation, and copy stages entirely and are carried to
the delete stage at the end, where each is offered for deletion
individually.

Because a view's SQL is the only thing lost when it is dropped, the
definition is printed in full immediately above each prompt (read via
`bq show --view` / `--materialized_view`, whichever the type requires).
Save it if you may need it, then confirm by typing the view name.
Nothing is written to disk for you — the definition is shown, not
archived.

A selection containing only views is valid: the run skips straight to
the view delete prompts. Views are also still offered when the table
side of a mixed selection aborts (every destination already exists, or
you decline the copy) — an aborted archive ends the archiving, not the
run.

STAGES

1. Pick        fzf multi-select over the shared bq-open cache.
2. Diagnostics three queries per table (see Query A/B/C below).
3. Confirm     a summary table listing every selected table with its
               row count, logical size, and DATA UPDATED timestamp
               (storage_last_modified_time — when the data was last
               written, not when metadata was touched). Destinations
               are probed first and any that already exist are listed
               in red and excluded from the run, so collisions are
               visible before you commit rather than surfacing as
               mid-copy failures. Requires typing 'yes'.
4. Copy        `bq cp --no_clobber` per table.
5. Verify      SELECT COUNT(*) on both source and archive. A match is
               required to reach stage 6. TABLE_STORAGE logical bytes
               are also compared but are advisory only — storage stats
               lag writes by minutes, so a fresh copy legitimately
               reports 0 for a while and gating on it would block
               every delete on a fast run.
6. Delete      one prompt per verified table, showing its row count and
               size, then one prompt per selected view, showing its SQL
               definition. Confirmation is the bare table/view name,
               not 'yes': the prompts are near-identical and repeated N
               times, which is precisely the shape that trains you to
               type the magic word without reading it. Confirming runs
               `bq rm --force --table` immediately. A table whose
               verification failed is never prompted for; views have no
               verification gate because nothing was copied.

Deleted refs are appended to ~/.cache/bq-open/dead-refs.txt so the
shared GC prunes them from every rlm-bq-* picker on the next run.

Any source that was NOT deleted — declined at the prompt, blocked by
failed verification, or whose `bq rm` failed — gets a `DROP TABLE` SQL
block (plus `bq rm` equivalents and queries to locate downstream views
and routines) printed to stdout AND written to a timestamped file
under $TMPDIR/bq-archive-drop-<timestamp>.sql. Tables already deleted
in stage 6 are omitted from that file. When every copied source was
deleted, no file is written.

Exit status is non-zero when a copy failed, a destination already
existed, the copy was declined, or a confirmed delete (table or view)
failed.

The picker reuses the `~/.cache/bq-open/` cache — run `bq-open` once
first to populate it. The cache key is derived from BQ_SEARCH_PATH +
SANDBOX_BQ_PROJECT + SANDBOX_BQ_DATASETS (the same algorithm
rlm-bq-open uses), so changes to those env vars bust the cache for
both tools at once.

The first one or two lines of the picker are REFRESH sentinels:
  --- REFRESH CACHE (cached <age>) ---
  --- REFRESH SANDBOX CACHE (cached <age>) ---   (sandbox only)
Selecting either one refreshes the corresponding cache file(s) and
re-invokes bq-archive with a fresh picker. Picking a REFRESH line
takes precedence over any real refs picked alongside it.

Key bindings:
  TAB / Shift-TAB  toggle selection
  Ctrl-P           cycle preview height (tall -> short -> hidden)
  Enter            confirm selection and proceed to diagnostics

Confirmation requires typing 'yes' before any copy happens, and the
exact table name before each individual delete.

ENVIRONMENT
  BQ_SEARCH_PATH
      Required. Colon-separated GCP project IDs; same as bq-open.

  BQ_ARCHIVE_QUERY_PROJECT
      Optional. GCP project where the three diagnostic queries run
      (must have region-eu INFORMATION_SCHEMA.JOBS, .TABLES,
      .TABLE_STORAGE, and a bq_log.cloudaudit_googleapis_com_data_access
      table). Default: the source project of the selected table.

  BQ_ARCHIVE_TARGET
      Optional. project.dataset to which the archive copies are
      written. Default: <source_project>.archival_data.

  BQ_ARCHIVE_LOOKBACK_DAYS
      Optional. Interval (in days) for the JOBS + audit queries.
      Default: 7.

  BQ_ARCHIVE_IGNORED_PRINCIPALS
      Optional, colon-separated audit-log principals excluded from
      Query B (typically scheduled-job service accounts whose hits
      are noise). Default: composer@... and data-build-tool@...
      service accounts in storytel-data-platform-prod.

REQUIRES
  bq, fzf, jq
